om zero:** `2.5` → `3` and `-2.5` → `-3`. The rounding uses the standard-library `decimal` module.\n- **Floats are rounded as the number they display as**, so `round_number(2.675, 2)` gives `2.68`. Python's built-in `round` gives `2.67` there, so this difference is intended.\n- **Return type:** a whole number (`int`) when `ndigits` is 0 or negative, and a `float` when it is positive. A negative `ndigits` rounds to tens, hundreds and so on: `(1250, -2)` → `1300`.\n- **Bad input:**\n  - `TypeError` if `value` isn't an int or float (including `True`/`False`).\n  - `TypeError` if `ndigits` isn't an int (including `True`/`False`).\n  - `ValueError` for NaN or infinity.\n- **Very large inputs and many decimal places** (`10**30`, `1e300`, `(0.1, 50)`) work without errors. The extra precision this needs is set only for that one calculation, and Python's global `decimal` settings are not changed.\n- `health_check` and `add` are unchanged, and nothing outside `src/` was edited apart from the summary file below.\n\nTo check the behaviour, I ran the design's worked examples and the error cases by hand in the interpreter, and all gave the expected results. Those checks were not saved as tests.\n\nI recorded the change in `.dark-factory/runs/e60eb29f-c3d2-44f4-9f43-99eebdccd5cb/artifacts/DEVELOPMENT/implementation_summary.json`.","ttft_ms":1726,"type":"result","duration_ms":34783,"uuid":"1303b2da-fe56-4374-9af8-829222248e24","ttft_stream_ms":894,"time_to_request_ms":243,"first_content_frame_ms":894,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
